bound by flame is a mess it's a mess  with high-minded ideas but it's a mess  all the same by the time the game comes  to a close it's clear that there's a  wide gulf between the experience you  just had and the vision that inspired it  bound by flame is a fantasy role-playing  game about choice the game presents you  with narrative decisions now and again  that effect faction allegiances and  spawn conflicts most of which can only  be solved through swordplay the star is  a mercenary called Vulcan he or she if  you choose a female avatar finds himself  sharing his brain with a growling demon  Vulcans mind becomes rather overcrowded  as a result but at least the demon  grants him the ability to conjure fire  bound by flames most intriguing choices  are those that see you deciding whether  to bends to the demons whims or assert  your humanity the more you side with the  demons advice the more you take on  demonic traits as I face the final  battle I had three sets of curled horns  growing from my blackened head and my  voice had taken on a diabolical Rumble  the consequences of these decisions  aren't just cosmetic growing horns for  example means you can't wear helmets  though you do gain enhanced magic power  regeneration by way of compensation  there are other trade-offs as well but  mutating into a fiery fiend is  nonetheless of delectable experience  there are choices to make and bound by  flames story that have nothing to do  with the demon within but the game's  poor dialogue and bland acting aren't  apt to make you care about anyone's fate  but your own well your ice Lords have  never messed with a powder Master now  have they  if you listen to the rabble who actually  live in the swamps we should head back  to the village now characters are  introduced one after another and many of  them are available as battle companions  they have tales to tell if you ask them  but bare-bones writing communicates only  the essential information without  fleshing out the people sharing it over  time narrative problems compound so  greatly that you can no longer take  anything seriously transitions from  gameplay to cut scenes are jarring with  characters repositioned instantly the  tone seesaws wildly with your  conversation partners cracking jokes at  the oddest moments and Vulcans suddenly  acting like a jerk for no apparent  reason when the final hours asked me to  make a sacrifice I made the decision  arbitrarily because none of this  insanity mattered to me your companions  aren't just boring they're also useless  in battle often following in a matter of  seconds  combat is bound by flames biggest  problem that the fighting is initially  pleasing you can switch between various  stances that let you use a formidable  two-handed weapon wield a pair of  daggers or sneak around and take on dead  enemies by surprise you can also toss  fireballs and infuse your weapon with  glowing flames thanks to your demon  friend  Vulcans muscular swings give combat a  nice sense of heft and the game skill  trees let you customize your fighting  style in sensible ways found by flame  then proceeds to ruin it's promising  combat with a series of choices that  suck out all the fun small areas are  overpopulated with ice spirits lurching  archers and other enemies with  ridiculously large health bars throw  into the mix of companion that you can't  revive in the middle of battle an  unreliable target lock incoming attacks  that always cause interrupts or knock  backs and a camera that can always frame  the action and you rarely find a good  fighting rhythm combat then becomes a  matter of exploiting defects not one of  finding a proper tempo you circle around  bosses for over a minute until your  health replenishes you run away from  battle so that your companion will come  back to life and you try your best not  to lead enemies from their aggravatingly  small leash  ranges lest they return to their homes  and regain all their health  you can help things along by placing  explosive traps and getting your parry  timing down pat but battles remain a  drag all the way up to the  excruciatingly final boss in between  battles you might pursue the game's few  side quests all of which sends you back  into the same lands corridors in which  the main quests occur you may also check  out the game's crafting system which is  its most robust feature various elements  can be combined to make useful items  like health potions or to bolster your  equipment refreshingly you can stop the  craft at any time even in the middle of  combat by RPG standards bound by flame  isn't a very long or expansive game if  you don't consider the battles you must  repeat the adventurers around a dozen  hours or so and there's no true world to  explore unless you consider a few medium  sized levels made up of narrow hallways  to be a world gone by flame has a lot of  ideas but never gives them room to grow  fear not my young friend I don't eat  elves they play havoc with my digestion  so you're not at any risk for me
 

